WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.904
Somewhere behind this process there is a real mean.

2
00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:08.284
Nobody on a shop floor has ever seen one.

3
00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:12.661
So take five parts and average them. That average is

4
00:00:12.761 --> 00:00:16.017
an estimate, and it is wrong — a little. Take

5
00:00:16.117 --> 00:00:19.895
another five, and it is wrong by a different amount.

6
00:00:19.983 --> 00:00:23.391
Keep going, and the estimates make a shape of their

7
00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:26.554
own. It is narrower than the parts, and it has

8
00:00:26.654 --> 00:00:30.131
a width you can predict before you measure anything.

9
00:00:30.216 --> 00:00:34.940
Sigma over root n. Level 4 derived it; here it is the size of

10
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.764
being wrong, and it is the thing an interval is built out of.

11
00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.249
An interval is the estimate plus and minus a margin. Build one from

12
00:00:47.349 --> 00:00:52.390
every sample and the interval is what moves — the truth does not.

13
00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:57.026
Green caught the real mean. Red missed it entirely — and on

14
00:00:57.126 --> 00:01:01.451
the shop floor you would never know which one you were holding.

15
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.961
That is all a confidence level is: run the procedure many times

16
00:01:06.061 --> 00:01:10.329
and this fraction of the intervals contain the truth. It is a

17
00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:14.626
property of the procedure, not of the interval in your hand.

18
00:01:17.016 --> 00:01:21.907
Here is where the textbooks quietly cheat. Sigma is never known

19
00:01:22.007 --> 00:01:25.868
either — it is estimated from the same five parts.

20
00:01:25.966 --> 00:01:29.759
Use one point nine six anyway, and count. At

21
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:34.890
five parts the interval that says ninety-five delivers 88.

22
00:01:34.983 --> 00:01:40.234
The margin is too narrow because s is itself uncertain. Replace the

23
00:01:40.334 --> 00:01:45.107
quantile with one that knows how few parts it was built from.

24
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.510
At five parts that quantile is 2.78, not 1.96 — and the

25
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:57.004
count lands on ninety-five where it belongs, at every sample size.

26
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.790
t is not a correction bolted onto a normal. It is

27
00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.664
what the arithmetic gives you when the spread is estimated too.

28
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:12.410
So how many parts does it take to be sure? Hold sigma known

29
00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:17.097
for a moment, so the quantile stops moving and only the root n

30
00:02:17.197 --> 00:02:21.558
is left. The width answers it, and the answer is expensive.

31
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.534
Watch the width fall as the parts arrive. It

32
00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:29.178
falls fast at first, and then it stops paying.

33
00:02:29.266 --> 00:02:34.422
Halve the width and the bill is four times the parts: five becomes

34
00:02:34.522 --> 00:02:39.678
twenty. Not ten — four times, because the error falls as the root.

35
00:02:39.766 --> 00:02:44.066
Certainty is bought by the square. That is the sentence to take

36
00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:48.186
to anyone who asks for a tighter number without more parts.

37
00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:55.251
One last place to point this. Every control chart in the rest

38
00:02:55.351 --> 00:03:00.439
of this curriculum multiplies a range by a constant called d two.

39
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:03.757
This site does not look it up. It simulates

40
00:03:03.857 --> 00:03:07.081
it — and anything simulated is an estimate.

41
00:03:07.166 --> 00:03:12.018
Run that simulation sixty times over and the answers scatter.

42
00:03:12.118 --> 00:03:16.402
The scatter is the standard error of our own constant.

43
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.786
Which sets a limit on honesty. Earning the third decimal

44
00:03:20.886 --> 00:03:25.486
of that constant from simulation alone would take 11 million

45
00:03:25.586 --> 00:03:30.030
subgroups. The fourth figure in the table is not simulated

46
00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:33.320
at all — it comes from the exact integral.

47
00:03:33.416 --> 00:03:37.071
So when Level 6 builds limits and Level 8 divides

48
00:03:37.171 --> 00:03:41.286
by a spread, remember what they are made of. Estimates,

49
00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:44.428
with a size you now know how to work out.

